# Previews: next steps

Generated by `maul setup previews` (maul {{.Version}}) for the **{{.EdgeTitle}}** recipe on **{{.Platform}}**.
Previews will be served at `https://<project>-<issue>.{{.Suffix}}`, and viewers sign in at `https://{{.SignIn}}`.
Nothing here has been applied: run the steps below in order. Running `maul setup previews` again shows what would
change before it replaces a file, and never draws a new Dex client secret unless you pass
`--rotate dex-client-secret`.

`maul setup previews -n {{.Namespace}} --apply --values <your values> --chart-version <version>` runs the in-cluster
steps below for you, in order, with your kubeconfig and helm, stopping at the first failure (add `--dry-run` to list
them first). Preview nodes, image pulls, Dex's static client, the DNS records{{if .Tailscale}} and the tailnet policy,
which must be saved before it runs,{{end}} stay yours.

## Files

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
{{- range .Files}}
| `{{.Path}}` | {{.What}} |
{{- end}}

The files under `secrets/` (mode 0600) hold the only copy of the Dex client secret{{if .DNSCredentialWritten}} and of
the DNS credential you gave{{end}}{{if .Cloudflare}} and of the tunnel credentials{{end}}{{if .TailscaleOAuthWritten}}
and of the Tailscale operator's OAuth client{{end}}. Apply them, or encrypt them (sops,
sealed-secrets) before they go anywhere else; never commit them as they are.

## 1. Before you start

- maul is installed in namespace `{{.Namespace}}` as release `{{.Release}}`, with `intentController.enabled: true`
  in your values: previews are built for intents, and the preview-controller needs intent-controller.
- NetworkPolicy is enforced. {{.NetworkPolicy}}
{{- if .SharedSite}}
- You accepted previews on `{{.SharedSite}}`, the registrable domain {{.SharedWith}} {{if .SharedBoth}}are{{else}}is{{end}} on
  (`--shared-domain acknowledged`), so preview code runs same-site, never same-origin, with {{if .SharedBoth}}them{{else}}it{{end}}.
  maul's own side holds: its cookies are `__Host-` (no preview can set or overwrite one) and its sign-in endpoints
  refuse a same-site request that is not a top-level navigation. What it cannot hold is every other app on
  `{{.SharedSite}}`: a preview can set cookies for all of `{{.SharedSite}}` and send same-site requests, so no app there
  may rely on a parent-domain cookie or on SameSite for CSRF{{if .SharedDex}}, Dex included (its cookies and forms are
  its own){{end}}. The values file records the domain (`previewAuth.sharedSiteAcknowledged: {{.SharedSite}}`), and
  `maul check previews` reports the sharing as INFO. See "Previews on the sign-in host's domain" in
  `docs/configuration/preview-gateway.md`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .GatewayCert}}
- {{if .CertManager}}cert-manager is installed in step 5 from `{{.CertManager.ValuesFile}}` (chart {{.CertManager.Version}}),
  unless it already runs: then run the command again with `--cert-manager-installed`.{{else}}cert-manager runs in the
  cluster (`--cert-manager-installed`).{{end}}
{{- if .Tailscale}}
- {{if .TailscaleOperator}}The Tailscale operator is installed in step 6 from `{{.TailscaleOperator.ValuesFile}}` (chart
  {{.TailscaleOperator.Version}}), unless it already runs: then run the command again with
  `--tailscale-operator-installed`.{{else}}The Tailscale operator runs in the cluster (`--tailscale-operator-installed`),
  and its tag `{{.TailscaleOperatorTag}}` owns `{{.TailscaleTag}}` in the tailnet policy (step 6).{{end}}
{{- end}}
{{- if not .Issuer}}
- Your {{.IssuerKind}} `{{.IssuerName}}`{{if eq .IssuerKind "Issuer"}} in namespace `{{.Namespace}}`{{end}} can answer an
  ACME DNS-01 challenge for both `*.{{.Suffix}}` and `{{.SignIn}}` (`--dns manual`).
{{- end}}
{{- else if .Cloudflare}}
{{- if .SignInShared}}
- Both names sit on the zone `{{.SuffixSite}}` in your Cloudflare account, the registrable domain you accepted previews
  sharing with sign-in (above). The sign-in host must stay outside `{{.Suffix}}`, so every preview host is a
  second-level name, which only an advanced certificate covers (`--cloudflare-acm`, step 5).
{{- else}}
- Both names sit on zones in your Cloudflare account: `{{.SuffixSite}}` for the previews and `{{.SignInSite}}` for the
  sign-in host. Two registrable domains keep preview code off sign-in's site; with only one domain, register a second,
  cheap one for previews, or accept sharing it with `--shared-domain acknowledged`. A preview suffix that is a whole
  zone is also what lets Cloudflare's free certificate cover the previews.
{{- end}}
- The locally-managed tunnel `{{.TunnelID}}` exists. You created it on your workstation, where its login certificate
  stays (`cert.pem` never enters the cluster):

  ```sh
  cloudflared tunnel login
  cloudflared tunnel create {{.TunnelName}}
  ```

  and this command read its credentials file (`~/.cloudflared/{{.TunnelID}}.json`) into `{{.CloudflaredSecretFile}}`.
- Cloudflare's edge terminates TLS for both names, so Cloudflare sees preview traffic in the clear. Previews should
  hold staging data only.
{{- else if .Certificate}}
- cert-manager runs in the cluster, and the {{.IssuerKind}} `{{.IssuerName}}`{{if eq .IssuerKind "Issuer"}} in
  namespace `{{.Namespace}}`{{end}} can answer an ACME DNS-01 challenge for both `*.{{.Suffix}}` and `{{.SignIn}}`
  (a wildcard needs DNS-01). {{if .SignInShared}}Both names sit on `{{.SharedSite}}`, usually in one DNS zone (two if
  `{{.Suffix}}` is delegated): the solver's credential must answer for each.{{else}}The two names sit on different registrable domains, so usually in two DNS zones: the
  solver's credential must answer for both.{{end}} See "The wildcard certificate" in
  `docs/configuration/preview-gateway.md` for an Issuer to start from.
{{- end}}

